## Testing standards

This document owns test selection, ownership, structure, assertions, fixture design
and conservative pruning. `validation.mtf` owns completion gates and commands.

The goal is high-confidence language and artifact correctness with the smallest
maintainable test footprint. Tests must protect distinct, credible failures, not
increase counts or cover syntax for its own sake. Rich Moth integration programs
stress interactions through the real compiler and runtime. Focused Rust unit
tests protect local invariants, individual failure paths and diagnostics that a
large program cannot isolate reliably. Neither test level replaces the other.

### Task-reading guide for testing

Read this document in full for test infrastructure, suite policy and broad audits.
Otherwise read the relevant sections through the next heading of the same or
higher level, including subsections.

[table:
    [th:
        [: **Task**]
        [: **Read**]
    ]
    [td:
        [: Choosing, adding, or locating tests]
        [: `Admission and ownership`, `Unit tests`, `Integration cases` and `Test location and module layout`]
    ]
    [td:
        [: New compiler or backend behavior]
        [: `Rich integration scenarios`, `Successful backend intent`, `Runtime output assertions` and `Backend matrices and artifact assertions`]
    ]
    [td:
        [: Diagnostics or hidden IR facts]
        [: `Diagnostics assertions` and `HIR and internal-IR assertions`]
    ]
    [td:
        [: Runner inventory, selection or suite policy]
        [: `Successful backend intent` and `Focused integration selection and inventory`]
    ]
    [td:
        [: Feature, platform or timing behavior]
        [: `Feature and platform lanes` and `Concurrency and repetition`]
    ]
    [td:
        [: Source audits, fixtures or performance]
        [: `Source-text assertions`, `Fixtures and temporary files` and `Benchmarks are not correctness tests`]
    ]
    [td:
        [: Review or removal of existing tests]
        [: `Pruning and duplicate coverage` and `Testing checklist`]
    ]
]

### Admission and ownership

Before writing a test, inspect nearby unit tests, related subsystems and the
canonical `tests/cases/` inventory. Identify the existing primary owner and
ask whether extending it would expose the same failure. Prefer, in order:

1. Extend an existing test or scenario with a meaningful new interaction or assertion.
2. Consolidate overlapping tests into one clearer test with all distinct failure paths retained.
3. Add a new test only for a credible regression that neither option can protect well.

A proposed test must have a clear answer to **what realistic bug would fail this
test, which assertion would catch it and why existing tests would miss it**.
Reject cases that merely vary literals, names, syntax spelling or setup without
changing a semantic boundary or failure path. A new test should add enough
regression value to justify its source, setup, maintenance and execution cost.
Keep tests understandable and deterministic. Do not build fixture frameworks or
shared helpers for a few callers. Do not widen the suite solely to raise a
coverage metric.

Choose the primary owner by the contract being protected:

[table:
    [th:
        [: **Contract**]
        [: **Primary owner**]
    ]
    [td:
        [: Pure algorithms, parser decisions, diagnostic reasons, impossible states and side-table invariants]
        [: Focused unit test close to the owning subsystem]
    ]
    [td:
        [: Language semantics, multi-feature behavior and user-visible project results]
        [: Real Moth scenario in `tests/cases/`]
    ]
    [td:
        [: Compiler-stage orchestration]
        [: Minimal pipeline/build test when the boundary itself is not already exercised]
    ]
    [td:
        [: Backend-specific artifact structure or target validation]
        [: Narrow artifact assertion or contractual golden]
    ]
    [td:
        [: Cross-backend parity]
        [: One Moth input with distinct backend assertions]
    ]
]

A contract may be exercised incidentally by many integration scenarios without
making each scenario its primary owner. Keep secondary tests only when they
protect a different stage, failure mechanism, diagnostic, target or observable
interaction. Check the actual assertions, not just matching feature names.

### Test location and module layout

- Tests live outside production implementation files. Put module-specific Rust
  tests under the module's `tests/` directory, for example
  `src/compiler_frontend/hir/tests/`.
- Rust harness and cross-stage tests belong under `src/compiler_tests/` when a
  smaller module owner is insufficient.
- Language and project integration scenarios belong under `tests/cases/`.
- Keep fixture data and test-only helpers with their owner. Share a helper only
  across genuine repeated use, without hiding the meaningful source shape.
- Use shared `portable_path_text` for asserted or displayed paths. Keep
  `Path` and `PathBuf` for filesystem operations and normalize only at the
  assertion/display boundary.

### MON and shared lexical coverage

`crates/moth-lexical/src/tests/` and `crates/moth-lexical/src/numeric/tests/`
own shared identifier, word and numeric leaf invariants.
`crates/moth-mon/src/tests/` owns codec-local invariants.
`crates/moth-mon/tests/` owns the public standalone API, exact numeric schemas,
raw numeric data, limits and errors. The compiler keeps only focused convenience
re-export identity coverage rather than a second copy of the public codec suite.

`src/compiler_tests/mon_syntax_parity/` compares real Moth source parsing
with independently decoded MON using equivalent types/schemas. Accepted cases
assert typed values before comparison. Invalid cases assert each parser's own
rejection reason and span. Never compare fabricated tokens or helper outputs.
`gaps.rs` documents and independently checks framing, nominal identity,
literal-data/expression differences, quoted text and resource limits. Keep
undelivered `{=}`, Unicode escapes and contextual `::Variant` linked to the
roadmap and progress matrix. Moth-native `$mon`, automatic schemas and the
static builder remain separate future work.

Use package-scoped commands so standalone tests don't depend on compiler feature
unification:

[codeblock, $code("bash"):
    cargo test -p moth-lexical
    cargo test -p moth-mon
    cargo test -p moth-mon --doc
    cargo test -p moth-lexical --doc
    cargo test -p moth --lib mon_syntax_parity
]

### Unit tests

Use focused Rust tests for behavior best isolated at the subsystem boundary:
pure algorithms, tokenization and parsing distinctions, precise diagnostics,
negative and overflow boundaries, malformed HIR, borrow/ownership proofs,
side-table facts, property tests and oracle or differential checks. These can
protect edge cases a successful end-to-end program cannot reach or distinguish.

A good unit test names one invariant, exercises its relevant success and failure
boundaries and asserts facts at the layer that owns them. Use compact table-driven
cases for closely related inputs when each row remains identifiable on failure.
Reuse local fixtures, but avoid large construction scaffolds and tests that
assert incidental representation or call order. Preserve focused regression
tests for real bugs even when a rich integration scenario also touches the
feature. Prune implementation-specific tests only after proving their contract
is obsolete or protected at the same required boundary elsewhere.

### Integration cases

`moth tests` runs the manifest-driven integration runner. Each canonical case is
one self-contained scenario under `tests/cases/`, with all helper source files
inside its `input/` directory. Helpers must not become standalone cases.

Integration tests should favor **richness over specificity**. Write real,
purposeful mini programs or deliberately adversarial Moth programs that exercise
several related language features together through parsing, typing, lowering,
borrow validation, target selection and execution. Stress surprising
interactions rather than repeating the simplest legal syntax form. A basic
`if` belongs in a focused unit test; a nested decision that calls functions,
mutates data, joins control flow and returns or handles an error belongs in a
larger scenario.

Protect observable results, not merely successful compilation. Prefer
deterministic state transitions, exact outputs, ordered events, error outcomes
and invariants that fail when the computed result is wrong. Use top-level
templates for output. Use `io.line(...)` only when console behavior matters.
Avoid goldens or emitted-code snapshots when runtime behavior is the contract.

### Rich integration scenarios

Useful scenarios include a small inventory/checkout engine, route planner,
record processor, task scheduler, parser for a tiny data format or deterministic
game simulation. They should have a purpose and deliberately combine:

- Nested branches, loop exits, value-producing control flow and function calls.
- Structs, choice variants, generics, collections and changing state.
- Error or optional propagation/recovery across function boundaries.
- Aliasing, mutation, copies, last uses and calls across modules when relevant.
- Constant folding, templates, numeric types and backend behavior where relevant.
- Edge cases such as empty collections, alternate branches, failed operations,
  boundary values and repeated updates within the same run.

For example, a logistics simulation can validate conservation across several
mutating functions rather than merely checking that each function compiles:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    -- Excerpt from a larger scenario, not a standalone fixture.
    validate_state |game Simulation|:
        carried ~= 0
        loop game.agents |agent|:
            if agent.health > 0:
                assert(agent.tile >= 0 and agent.tile < board_cells)
            ;
            ~carried += agent.cargo
        ;
        mined = counter_value(game.counters, "mined")
        delivered = counter_value(game.counters, "delivered")
        lost = counter_value(game.counters, "lost")
        assert(mined is delivered + lost + carried)
    ;
]

A good scenario has multiple independently observable outcomes and a few
meaningful invariant checks, so a compiler bug is likely to produce a visible
mismatch even if execution completes. Include at least one path that is
*not* the trivial happy path. Negative compile-time diagnostics need separate
focused failure cases because a rejected program cannot execute the rest of
its intended scenario.

Aim for a cohesive mini program, not an exhaustive language sampler. The
attached Ember Relay simulation is an exceptional upper bound: it demonstrates
how realistic interacting logic finds bugs missed by shallow tests, but its
scale, canvas presentation and feature breadth are not targets for ordinary
new cases. Prefer smaller scenarios with identifiable failure surfaces. When
a scenario becomes difficult to diagnose, split it by coherent behavior, not
by individual syntax construct. Favor enriching an existing relevant scenario
over adding another trivial standalone fixture.

For new language syntax, verify that the *suite as a whole* covers applicable
positive use, negative type/namespace/visibility cases, duplicates,
cross-file behavior and target rejection. Add only missing distinct contracts,
and place precise negative cases near their existing owners.

### Successful backend intent

Every successful HTML and HTML-Wasm backend run applies its universal backend baseline. The
baseline protects harness and target invariants. It is not the case's authored semantic contract.

Use `success_contract = "acceptance_only"` only when that backend has no stronger case-specific
semantic, artifact, golden, absence or expected-warning assertion.

[codeblock, $code("toml"):
    [backends.html]
    mode = "success"
    warnings = "forbid"
    success_contract = "acceptance_only"
]

Acceptance-only intent does not disable the baseline. The audit records both
`baseline_applied = true` and `acceptance_only = true`, with `backend_baseline` and
`acceptance_only` in `assertion_kinds`.

Do not combine acceptance-only intent with artifact assertions, golden mode, rendered-output
assertions, artifact-absence assertions or an authored expected-warning contract. A whole case
whose backends are all acceptance-only uses `role = "smoke"`. A mixed-backend case may use a
boundary or backend role when another backend owns a stronger contract.

A successful backend with only the universal baseline is invalid. Typed fixture loading rejects it
before policy evaluation, listing, audit, or execution.

Canonical fixture shape:

[codeblock, $code("text"):
    tests/cases/
    +-- manifest.toml
    +-- case_name/
        +-- input/
        |   +-- main.moth
        |   +-- helper.moth
        +-- expect.toml
        +-- golden/
            +-- html/
            +-- html_wasm/
]

- `manifest.toml` is the authoritative case order. Every case declares a unique canonical `id`,
  unique fixture `path` and at least one non-empty tag.
- Manifest case paths are relative and may not contain absolute, platform-prefix, parent or
  current-directory components. Canonicalised fixture roots must remain inside `tests/cases/`,
  including through symlinks.
- IDs, paths, tags, contracts, roles and configured entries reject leading or trailing whitespace.
  Tags within one case must be unique.
- Every canonical case declares a `role`. Supported roles are `primary`, `boundary`, `backend`,
  `adversarial` and `smoke`.
- Every non-smoke case declares a `contract`. A whole-case acceptance-only smoke may remain
  contractless because it has no case-specific semantic owner. Do not invent a contract for it.
- A `primary` case requires a contract, and one contract may have at most one primary case.
- A contract family without a primary is reported once. Backend-only and
  adversarial-only families are advisories; other secondary-only families still require review.
- Case IDs, tags, contracts and roles are retained as typed runner metadata. Do not reconstruct
  them from display text or fixture paths.

Manifest entry shape:

[codeblock, $code("toml"):
    [[case]]
    id = "borrow_conflict_resolved_by_reordering"
    path = "borrow_conflict_resolved_by_reordering"
    tags = ["integration", "borrows"]
    contract = "language.bindings.alias_final_use_allows_mutable_rebinding"
    role = "primary"
]

### Focused integration selection and inventory

Use the canonical case ID and retained metadata for local iteration:

[codeblock, $code("bash"):
    cargo run --quiet -- tests --case arithmetic_operator_precedence --backend html
    cargo run --quiet -- tests --tag borrows --tag diagnostics --backend html
    cargo run --quiet -- tests --contract <contract-id>
    cargo run --quiet -- tests --list --tag borrows
]

- `--case` and `--contract` are exact matches.
- Repeatable `--tag` filters compose as logical AND.
- Case, tag, contract and backend filters compose and preserve manifest/backend order.
- `--list` accepts selection filters and reports canonical ID, selected backend blocks, tags,
  contract and role without compiling cases.
- Duplicate singleton options, duplicate tag values, missing values, unknown flags and positional
  arguments are errors.

Use the audit mode to validate and inventory the complete canonical suite without compiling cases:

[codeblock, $code("text"):
    cargo run --quiet -- tests --audit
]

Audit writes its JSON inventory before returning hard-policy failure. Normal listing and execution
use the same policy evaluator and reject hard findings before compiling a case. Missing roles,
missing contracts on non-smoke cases, primary cases without contracts and duplicate primary owners
are hard findings. Contractless whole-case acceptance-only smoke cases are valid.

Audit cannot be combined with filters or `--list`. It writes the reporting-owned JSON inventory to
`target/test-reports/integration_suite_inventory.json`, including canonical metadata, backend
expectation facts, hard-policy violations and primary-less contract-family advisories. The report
is review input only; it never deletes, merges or rewrites fixtures.

The current inventory reports acceptance-only, rendered-output, artifact,
golden, absence, expected-warning, Wasm trap and uncaught Moth Error contracts,
including per-backend assertion kinds and the zero baseline-only invariant.
It distinguishes exact, ordered and exactly-once output, warning codes,
diagnostic match modes and any contains-mode justification. Weak-contract
review fields identify smoke roles, ignored warnings and contains-mode
diagnostics; they are review signals, not automatic rejection. A new weak
contract must be justified rather than inherited by accident.

Every report records the command, run ID, host OS/architecture, compiled
features, runner thread count, completion state and repository revision (or a
reason it is unavailable). Reports use atomic replacement. An interrupted run
leaves `completed = false`, not an old success. Audit output is evidence for
review, never permission to delete, merge or rewrite fixtures automatically.

### Runtime output assertions

Runtime assertions use one Node harness. It records console output and HTML fragment inserts in one
event array when each event happens, then joins their text with newlines in that same order. Channel
views are derived from the event array. They never reconstruct chronology after execution.

The harness runs only when a backend declares a rendered-output assertion. It executes the emitted
page scripts, captures pending page microtasks, and waits for Node to report unhandled Promise
rejections before emitting its summary. Do not make scheduler turns or incidental microtask counts
contractual.

Choose the narrowest field that owns the behaviour:

- `rendered_output_exact` protects the complete combined output and is mutually exclusive with other
  text-output fields. It may accompany `runtime_error_contains`, `runtime_trap_contains` or
  `entry_error_code`.
  Exact comparison normalizes line endings only and preserves every other whitespace difference.
- `rendered_output_contains_in_order` protects the relative order of two or more fragments.
- `rendered_output_contains_exactly_once` protects activation, mounting and helper output from
  duplication.
- `rendered_output_contains` and `rendered_output_not_contains` protect required and forbidden
  fragments when complete output isn't the contract.
- `runtime_error_contains` protects the message of an uncaught Moth runtime Error that ends page
  execution. Only values thrown as `new Error(...)` count. This field does not observe recovered
  `Error!` results, which need ordinary output assertions.
- `runtime_trap_contains` protects an uncaught `WebAssembly.RuntimeError` message on `html_wasm`.
  It requires success mode and at least one non-empty fragment. It cannot accompany
  `runtime_error_contains`: a host trap never satisfies a Moth Error expectation.
- `entry_error_code` expects a returned entry `Error` with exactly that `U32` code, including `0`.
  Compilation must succeed, so use `mode = "success"`. It cannot accompany `runtime_error_contains`
  or `runtime_trap_contains`. Generated start callers record the canonical code when they inspect
  the start result, so notice text, process status, host exceptions and Wasm traps cannot satisfy it.
  Later host output, faults or status changes cannot extend or erase this terminal outcome.
- Any terminal outcome ends the run. Text assertions check only output captured before it.
  An unrequested terminal outcome fails the case. Other engine errors, including `TypeError`,
  `WebAssembly.CompileError` and `WebAssembly.LinkError`, remain harness failures.

[codeblock, $code("toml"):
    [backends.html]
    mode = "success"
    warnings = "forbid"
    rendered_output_contains = ["active-root"]
    rendered_output_not_contains = ["imported-root"]
    rendered_output_contains_in_order = ["before-loop", "after-loop"]
    rendered_output_contains_exactly_once = ["active-root", "mounted"]
]

Use a returned-entry contract for an unchecked numeric failure propagated to start. Exact empty
output also protects success-only runtime fragment publication:

[codeblock, $code("toml"):
    [backends.html]
    mode = "success"
    warnings = "forbid"
    entry_error_code = 301
    rendered_output_exact = ""
]

Use `runtime_error_contains = ["assertion failed"]` for fatal Moth Error
conditions. Use `runtime_trap_contains = ["unreachable"]` on `html_wasm` for an
intended Wasm trap. Keep traps and Moth Errors distinct from unrelated host
exceptions or Wasm memory faults.

Use exact output for small deterministic behaviours such as loop or map operation results. Use
ordered and exact-once fragments when unrelated output may coexist. Prefer these runtime contracts
over generated JavaScript text or a whole-page golden when execution is the real owner.

An exact mismatch is reported escaped, post-normalization, with the byte offset of the first
difference, so a whitespace-only difference does not print as two identical lines.

#### What the harness will execute

The harness parses the emitted page for a supported script shape and rejects everything else rather
than guessing:
- a classic inline script is executed
- exactly one inline `type="module"` script is executed as a native ES module with
  its validated relative JS graph staged; mixed classic+module pages, multiple module
  scripts and any import map are rejected by name
- an external `src`, an unknown `type`, `nomodule`, `async`, and a malformed or unterminated tag are
  each rejected by name
- the page script include must appear exactly once inside the body
#### Harness failure classes

A harness problem is never reported as a case failure. `RenderHarnessError` names which boundary
broke: `Artifact`, `Workspace`, `Spawn`, `Timeout`, `ExitStatus`, `OutputDecoding`,
`OutputProtocol`, `ScriptShape` or `Cleanup`. Execution is bounded by a 30-second deadline with
kill-and-reap, stdout and stderr are drained on their own threads under a 4 MiB bound, and a stderr
decode failure never replaces the boundary that actually failed.

#### Goldens carry their own artifact kind

A golden file's extension decides the artifact kind it expects, and a cross-kind match is rejected
before content is read. Identical bytes emitted as a different artifact kind do not satisfy a
golden. Text goldens require strict UTF-8; invalid UTF-8 is a harness failure, not a rewrite.

### Concurrency and repetition

A test that waits for a thread to reach a state must observe that state, not guess how long
reaching it takes.

- Use a condition variable or a channel for an expected transition.
- Use a bounded wall-clock deadline only as deadlock protection, never as the thing being measured.
- A deadline failure must name the observed state, so the report says what did not happen.
- Join every spawned worker, so a panic inside one is the reported cause rather than a discarded
  result.
- Do not make an arbitrary sleep, a yield count or a machine-speed threshold part of a contract.

Stateful owners - the timing collector, the frontend counter stores, output writes,
current-directory guards and the Node harness - only misbehave under a thread schedule or a repeat
that one default-parallelism run never produces. That schedule has an owned command:

[codeblock, $code("bash"):
    just stress
]

It repeats the unit and integration suites at one thread, default parallelism and sixteen threads,
and keeps running after a failing lane so the whole matrix is visible.

### Diagnostics assertions

- Ordinary source/config/import/type/rule/borrow failures should assert stable `diagnostic_codes`.
- `diagnostic_codes` is an exact unordered multiset by default. Repeat a code when the diagnostic
  must occur more than once.
- Add rendered message fragments only when wording or related-site text is part of the contract.
- Infrastructure-boundary tests may assert each `InfrastructureFailureKind` and stage, ordered cause aggregation when relevant, and that infrastructure causes do not enter diagnostics.
- Compiler-bug tests catch the panic and assert the `CompilerBugReport` stage and message.
- Preserve source location assertions where location behavior is important.
- Tests of family construction and freezing should inspect typed family-view facts, stable code, severity, exact primary/related sites and report-owned identity where those are the contract.
- Lifetime-boundary tests should prove a frozen report remains renderable after its worker and source database have been dropped when that is the ownership contract; failed worker state never returns to the host.
- Do not use full rendered diagnostic snapshots when a stable code and structured assertion are sufficient.
- Do not weaken a diagnostic case into a generic "must fail" assertion.

[codeblock, $code("toml"):
    [backends.html]
    mode = "failure"
    warnings = "forbid"
    diagnostic_codes = ["MOTH-RULE-0033", "MOTH-RULE-0033"]
]

Use `diagnostic_assertions` when a broad code needs a compiler-owned reason or when source
remapping is part of the contract. Each table selects one diagnostic by stable code and one-based
occurrence. A unique code may omit `occurrence`. Repeat the code in `diagnostic_codes` to preserve
its exact multiplicity.

[codeblock, $code("toml"):
    [[backends.html.diagnostic_assertions]]
    code = "MOTH-RULE-0057"
    reason = "invalid_generic_instantiation.recursive_function_instantiation"
    path = "input/@page.moth"
    line = 3
    count = 1

    [[backends.html.diagnostic_assertions.secondary_labels]]
    occurrence = 1
    path = "input/helpers.moth"
    line = 2
]

- Reason keys come from the diagnostic family schema and typed family view. The runner must not invent a parallel
  reason taxonomy or derive identity from rendered text.
- Paths are normalized relative to the owning fixture and lines are one-based display lines.
- Assert columns only when exact token-span precision is the contract.
- A secondary-label occurrence is one-based among that diagnostic's secondary labels in emitted
  order. Assert only the related sites whose location or ordering is the behaviour owner.
- Keep `message_contains` only when rendered wording or related-site prose is itself contractual.

Use `diagnostic_match = "contains"` only when intentional independent recovery or cascades make
additional diagnostics part of the accepted current behaviour. Every contains-mode block needs a
substantive `diagnostic_match_reason`. The suite audit reports the reason and treats a missing or
blank reason as a hard policy finding.

[codeblock, $code("toml"):
    diagnostic_match = "contains"
    diagnostic_match_reason = "Independent recovery may emit additional diagnostics from another source unit."
]

Warnings use a separate backend-local contract:

- `warnings = "forbid"` rejects every warning and accepts no `warning_codes` field.
- `warnings = "ignore"` deliberately makes warnings non-contractual and accepts no
  `warning_codes` field.
- `warnings = "exact"` requires `warning_codes` as an exact unordered multiset. Repeat duplicate
  codes to preserve their required count.

[codeblock, $code("toml"):
    [backends.html]
    mode = "success"
    warnings = "exact"
    warning_codes = ["MOTH-RULE-0022", "MOTH-RULE-0022"]
    rendered_output_contains = ["warning-contract result=one"]
]

### Backend matrices and artifact assertions

Use one `expect.toml` per case with backend-specific assertion blocks.

[codeblock, $code("toml"):
    entry = "."

    [backends.html]
    mode = "success"
    warnings = "forbid"
    artifacts_must_not_exist = ["unexpected.html"]

    [backends.html_wasm]
    mode = "success"
    warnings = "forbid"

    [[backends.html_wasm.artifact_assertions]]
    path = "page.wasm"
    kind = "wasm"
    validate_wasm = true
    must_export = ["memory", "moth_str_ptr", "moth_str_len", "moth_release"]
]

Pair HTML-JS and `html_wasm` cases for supported exact scalar values, comparisons
and casts. Use @../../docs/numbers/#numeric-types (Numeric types) and
@../../docs/numbers/#operators (Operators) for semantic expectations, not a
profile matrix or a minimum arithmetic width. Keep numeric failures within the
ordinary diagnostic, recovered-output or returned-entry assertion contracts.
Assert the intended target rejection for unsupported paths. The
@../../docs/progress (Progress matrix) owns current backend support, including
the remaining Wasm gates.

- `entry = "."` is the one supported current-directory sentinel and selects the case's `input/`
  directory. Every other entry is a relative contained path with no absolute, platform-prefix,
  parent or current-directory component. Its canonical path must remain inside the owning
  `input/` directory, including through symlinks.
- Store strict goldens in:

[codeblock, $code("text"):
    golden/<backend>/
]

- Use artifact assertions for structure: file presence, Wasm validity, exports, imports, expected snippets, absence of forbidden output.
- Use `artifacts_must_not_exist` for success cases whose contract requires an exact normalized output path to be absent. Paths reported as `NotBuilt` are ignored because they were never emitted.
- Use strict goldens only where exact emitted text is contractual.
- The canonical suite currently has no file-backed golden blocks. A future golden needs an explicit
  exact-artifact rationale that a runtime or narrow artifact assertion cannot express.
- Golden discovery recursively counts actual files. Empty or directory-only golden trees do not
  create a contract. Nested files do.
- `golden_mode` without at least one discovered file is invalid. When files exist, strict is the
  default if no mode is authored. When no files exist, audit reports `golden_present = false` and
  `golden_mode = null`.
- Prefer semantic or rendered-output assertions for behavior-first cases.
- Strengthen artifact assertions when a golden can pass while structural behavior is wrong.
- Assert intended rejections for unsupported backends rather than forcing matrix symmetry.

### HIR and internal-IR assertions

- Assert semantic relationships rather than incidental indexes.
- Prefer branch joins, merge-local relationships, jump arguments, region ownership, side-table keys, stable call targets and required operation kinds.
- Avoid exact block IDs or vector offsets unless exact layout is the invariant.
- Do not make temporary lowering order part of the public test contract.
- For HIR validation, distinguish malformed-HIR invariant tests from user-facing source diagnostic tests.

Compact scalar-memory coverage lives in the Wasm emitter tests. It instantiates the normal
emitter's module in Node and inspects stored bytes, adjacent scalar slots, signed and unsigned
narrow reloads, and floating-point signed zero. This is evidence for the LIR memory-access
contract, not source-level aggregate layout, allocation or field/index support.

### Feature and platform lanes

A feature-gated test that no command runs is not a test. Every declared Cargo feature belongs to a
lane. Standard lanes run in the feature matrix. Deliberately expensive developer systems may use an
opt-in lane with its own named command:

[codeblock, $code("bash"):
    just test-feature-matrix
]

[table:
    [th:
        [: **Lane**]
        [: **Features**]
        [: **Only this lane covers**]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `default`]
        [: none]
        [: the shipped configuration and every `cfg(not(feature = ...))` branch]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `timers`]
        [: `timers`]
        [: the timing collector, boundary identities and command/build timing tests]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `detailed-timers`]
        [: `detailed_timers`]
        [: AST substage timings and the detailed-only summary shape]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `counters`]
        [: `benchmark_counters`]
        [: counter-only builds, where counters record without a timing collector]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `timers-counters`]
        [: `timers`, `benchmark_counters`, `data_layout_memory_probe`]
        [: the collector-backed counters and the retained-layout allocator probe]
    ]
    [td:
        [: `scoped-blocks`]
        [: `checked_blocks`, `async_blocks`]
        [: the deferred-feature diagnostics for `$checked:` and `$async:` blocks]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `dev-output`]
        [: every `show_*` feature]
        [: the developer stage-dump branches, which no other lane compiles]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `xtask`]
        [: none]
        [: the benchmark, profiling and process-runner tests in the `xtask` package]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `moth-lexical`]
        [: none]
        [: standalone shared lexical and numeric-text tests without compiler feature unification]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `moth-mon`]
        [: none]
        [: standalone codec unit, public consumer and documentation tests]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `boracle`]
        [: `boracle`]
        [: the reference-solver developer gate, owned by `just boracle` and excluded from the
        standard matrix]
    ]
    [td:
        [: `boracle-campaign`]
        [: `boracle`, `boracle_campaign`]
        [: the measured generated differential campaign, owned by `just boracle-campaign`: 2048
        deterministic generated shapes per cyclic mode, 4096 problems and 8192 comparisons,
        classified through the same differential comparison the `boracle` lane uses]
    ]
]

Lanes are package-scoped, and that is not a stylistic choice. Cargo unifies features across one
resolve graph, and `xtask` depends on `moth` with `features = ["timers"]`, so
`cargo test --workspace` always builds the compiler with `timers` enabled. It can never run the
default configuration, and `--features benchmark_counters` on a workspace command is really
`timers` plus counters. Only `cargo test -p moth` with an explicit feature list configures the
crate the way a lane claims.

Each compiler lane also has a matching `moth-cli` lane, named `cli-` followed
by the compiler lane name, with the same feature selection. Those lanes execute
command parsing, scaffolding and subprocess
contracts against the CLI's forwarded configuration. They do not replace
compiler unit tests. The CLI's Boracle and campaign lanes remain opt-in through
the same named recipes as their compiler counterparts.

Feature coverage is checked mechanically, without running a lane:

[codeblock, $code("bash"):
    just feature-lane-check
]

`just feature-lane-check` checks the compiler, CLI, lexical, MON and `xtask`
manifests against `cfg` references in their source and test directories. It
fails on uncovered or unknown features and missing roots. Its report at
`target/test-reports/feature_lane_coverage.json` maps features to lanes and
files, not outcomes. `just test-feature-matrix` records executed standard
lanes in `target/test-reports/feature_matrix_results.json`; `boracle` and
`boracle-campaign` run only through their named opt-in commands.

Adding a Cargo feature means adding it to a standard or opt-in lane in the same change. The gate
fails otherwise.

Platform-gated tests are owned by the platform that has the API:

[table:
    [th:
        [: **Gate**]
        [: **Runs on**]
        [: **Owns**]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `cfg(unix)`]
        [: Linux and macOS]
        [: symlink identity and retargeting, non-regular nodes, unreadable paths and non-UTF-8 names]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `cfg(windows)`]
        [: Windows]
        [: extended-length path prefixes, Windows canonicalisation and hard-link inspection]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `cfg(any(unix, windows))`]
        [: all three]
        [: the portable policy shared by both families, asserted on each]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `cfg(target_os = "linux")`]
        [: Linux]
        [: non-UTF-8 filesystem identity, which macOS rejects at the filesystem boundary]
    ]
]

CI runs every gate on Linux, macOS and Windows, so a platform-gated owner executes wherever its API
exists. A platform-specific test may be absent only when the underlying API does not exist there;
an equivalent portable policy test should still run everywhere. Windows results are currently
non-blocking - `validation.mtf` owns that status and the conditions that end it.

### Source-text assertions

A test that reads source text and asserts on it proves what the text says, not what the code does.
An alias, a reformat or an equivalent reimplementation all pass it.

- Do not make a source-text assertion the only evidence for runtime or semantic behaviour.
- A ban whose scope is "no file anywhere may contain this" belongs to `just source-audit`, which
  walks the tree once and reports typed findings. `validation.mtf > Source audit` owns it.
- Reading a non-Rust asset a test then parses or executes is not a source-text assertion. The asset
  is the input, and the behaviour under test is the parse or the execution.

### Fixtures and temporary files

- Prefer `tempfile::tempdir()` for temporary directories and files.
- Use unmanaged paths only when the test intentionally owns cleanup.
- Keep fixtures local to their owner.
- Do not commit generated `dev/` or `release/` outputs from test or benchmark projects.
- Avoid shared fixture builders that hide important source shape.

### Pruning and duplicate coverage

Pruning is an evidence-based behavior audit, not a line-count exercise. Similar
source text, repeated setup and a feature appearing in both suites do not by
themselves establish redundancy. For each candidate cluster:

1. Inventory its actual assertions and failure paths. Check related unit tests,
   `tests/cases/`, the suite inventory and `test_honesty_inventory.json`.
2. Mark each contract `keep`, `extend`, `merge`, `promote` or `remove`. State the
   retained owner and why it can still catch each distinct regression.
3. Consolidate equivalent local cases with a table or a small existing helper.
   Extend a meaningful integration scenario when the missing risk is an
   interaction visible through the full compiler.
4. Remove a unit test only when its behavior is obsolete or the surviving
   tests cover the same important boundary and failure paths. Preserve exact
   diagnostic, malformed-IR, algorithmic, property, oracle, borrow and numeric
   corner cases unless equivalent protection is demonstrated.
5. Change small clusters at a time. Record affected files, contracts, surviving
   test IDs, before/after test counts and noncomment LOC, plus any uncovered
   gaps. Run the focused tests and the relevant full integration, feature,
   platform and benchmark/CI lanes. Investigate regressions instead of
   weakening assertions.

Prefer deletion to new infrastructure. Keep small, clear duplicates when their
separate failure localization or boundary protection is valuable. There is no
mandatory LOC or test-count reduction target and no assumption that a longer
integration program supersedes focused units. The desired outcome is less
maintenance for stronger distinct regression protection.

### Benchmarks are not correctness tests

Benchmark fixtures are performance evidence, not correctness coverage.

Do not use benchmark coverage as a substitute for unit or integration tests.
Negative diagnostics belong in `tests/cases`, not benchmark fixtures.

Use `bench-check` to detect broad performance regressions after correctness has
already been established by ordinary tests.

### Testing checklist

Before accepting a test addition or cleanup, verify:

- Related unit and integration tests were inspected before adding new coverage.
- Every new test protects a credible distinct failure and has an assertion that
  would expose it. Extending an existing owner was considered first.
- Integration scenarios are realistic or adversarial, exercise nontrivial
  interactions and assert meaningful observable outcomes.
- Unit tests retain precise negative paths, private invariants and useful
  failure localization. A complex end-to-end case is not their automatic replacement.
- Diagnostics use stable codes/reasons. HIR assertions avoid incidental IDs.
- Runtime output and artifact checks match the actual contract. Goldens are
  reserved for cases where exact emitted bytes are important.
- Backend differences, multi-file ownership and feature/platform lanes are
  respected. Temporary paths have owned cleanup.
- A proposed removal has a documented surviving owner and preserves distinct
  edge cases and failure paths.
- No benchmark is counted as correctness coverage. Validation follows
  `validation.mtf` and weakened expectations are treated as regressions.
